{"text": "\"These data support the importance of never smoking or\nquitting if you do,\" said Dr. Kuller.\nThis segment of MRFIT was completed by researchers at the\nUniversity of Pittsburgh Graduate School of Public Health, the\nUniversity of Massachusetts Medical School and the University of\nMinnesota School of Public Health.\n\nCopyright NCI CANCER WEEKLY via NewsNet Monday September 17, 1990\n\nYale University\nSMOKING HOUSEHOLDS PUT YOUNGSTERS AT GREATER CANCER RISK\n\nYale University researchers, Dr. Dwight Janerich et al.,\nstudying the effects of passive smoking on the family, report up\nto a two-fold increase of lung cancer risk in children who are\nraised in smoke-filled homes where both parents smoke.\nThe study compared 191 lung cancer patients who did not\nsmoke against 191 non-smoking healthy individuals to determine\nthe average risk of passive smoke in home, work and social\nencounters.\n\u2018Their findings show almost 1 in 5 cases of lung cancer in\nnon-smokers can be linked to being raised around people who\nsmoke, and that long-term exposure appears to make a person more\nsusceptible to lung cancer later in life.\nJanerich says the results indicate that \"parents would be\nwell advised to try to avoid having tobacco smoke in the house.\u201d\nSmoking by a spouse or in the workplace does not appear to\nincrease the risk of lung cancer in non-smokers, according to the\nresearchers.\nStrangely, they found non-smokers were at less of a risk\nfrom passive smoke in social settings, according to Janerich.\n\u201cParents who smoke are harming their children,\" said Dr.\nRonald Davis, director of the U.S. Office on Smoking and Health.\n\u201cI they aren't causing emphysema and lung problems during their\nchildhood they're increasing their risk of cancer when they grow\n\n\u201cWhat is important about this study is its size,\" said Dr.\n\u2018Scott Weiss, a pediatric respiratory disease specialist at Beth\nIsrael Hospital in Boston. \"It is the largest study done so far\nof true non-smokers with a rigorous definition of non-smoking.\"\n\nSZETESIFV]",
